 "conclusions": {
  "patchable_aot_vs_stock_aot": "Mixed. Straight-line arithmetic is unchanged (numeric loop 1.00x, in-function loop 1.00x). Virtual/interface dispatch 3.3x, List/Map ops 6.7x, string building 2.9x. Every non-inlined static call or constructor costs ~75-80 ns in V1 vs ~1 ns in V0 (AOT->kernel call 76.5 vs 1.4 ns; kernel->AOT leaf 81 vs 1.0 ns), so allocation of small objects through constructors is 25.9x slower. Frame build time (UI thread) 2.80x (2.0 vs 0.72 ms median), total frame span 1.6x; no frame exceeded 8.4 ms at 120 Hz. App.framework 47 MB vs 2.5 MB.",
  "interpreter_slowdown": "Interpreted code runs ~19-20x slower than stock AOT on pure arithmetic (V2 20.0x, V3 18.9x), 17.5x on List/Map ops, 5x on string building (dominated by native StringBuffer), 44-50x for virtual calls (interpreter dispatch), 27-31x for allocation. Against patchable AOT (V1) the interpreter is 20x on arithmetic, 3.9x on List/Map, 1.7x on strings, 13x on virtual calls and 1.2x on allocation, because V1 itself pays the call overhead.",
  "transition_costs": "AOT calling a patched (interpreted) trivial function: 120 ns/call in V2 (vs 76.5 in V1 where it is an AOT call, 1.4 in V0). Interpreted function calling a trivial AOT function: 24 ns/call in V2 (V3 full interpreter: 18.7 ns). In V2 the AOT->interpreter transition therefore costs ~45 ns over the already-slow V1 call path; interpreter->AOT is cheaper than a V1 AOT->AOT call.",
  "frame_impact": "V0 build 0.72 ms median / 1.05 p90; V1 2.02/3.82; V2 (item build calls the interpreted kernel, 27 items/frame, 2000-iteration loop each) 2.30/4.00; V3 (everything interpreted, framework included) 5.80/6.25 ms, total span 5.97 ms vs 1.39 (4.3x), 6-9 of ~1200 frames over the 8.4 ms 120 Hz budget, none over 16.7 ms. This workload is light; raster durations are 0.07-0.08 ms for V1-V3 vs 0.43 ms for V0 (unexplained, not investigated).",
  "decision_notes": "Partial patching preserves AOT speed for unpatched arithmetic, but the structural patchable AOT mode taxes every non-inlined static call/constructor by ~75 ns and bloats the binary ~18x, which is the dominant cost; whether that overhead is inherent to indirect calls (an indirect call itself is a few ns) or an unoptimized call path in the patchable build was not investigated and would decide patchable-AOT vs a full optimizing linker. A full interpreter (V3) is ~19x slower on CPU code but, for this light UI workload, still kept all frames under 16.7 ms on an M4 iPad."
 },
 "caveats": [
  "Single physical device (iPad Pro 13-inch M4), 3 cold launches per variant; run-to-run spread is small (see run_medians).",
  "All kernels live in one patchable function patchedKernel(mode,n,Workset); in V2 the virtual/interface/alloc/collection kernels are interpreted but call unchanged AOT classes/collections (mixed-mode). V3 gives the pure-interpreter numbers.",
  "patchedKernel and aotLeaf carry @pragma('vm:never-inline') in every variant so call boundaries are real in V0.",
  "V1-V3 have auto_update enabled and perform a network patch check at startup (Local Network permission granted); V0 has no updater.",
  "Frame timings from SchedulerBinding.addTimingsCallback over 10 s after 3 s warm-up; screen kept awake via isIdleTimerDisabled. 120 Hz ProMotion display.",
  "Test AES key is embedded in V1-V3 builds via tool/with_key.sh (disposable, local server only); development signing with get-task-allow=true was needed for container copy-back."
 ]
}